INT. PAPER LANTERN FACTORY - EVENING

Hundreds of unlit lanterns hang from the rafters like pale moons. Long tables, glue pots, bamboo ribs. One window glows orange.

MEI-LING TSAI, 30s, in a paint-spattered smock, glues a seam. Her fiance, DANIEL OKORO, 30s, in a suit, stands at the door with a small box. MEI-LING's mother, AUNTIE FEN, 60s, folds paper at the next table. Her brother, JASPER TSAI, 20s, hauls a crate. Daniel's mother, GRACE OKORO, 60s, sits near the stove with tea. A FOREMAN, 50s, watches from the loft.

DANIEL
You said seven.

MEI-LING
(not looking up)
Lanterns don't glue themselves.

Daniel sets the box on the table. It sits there. Fen pretends not to look. Jasper drops the crate. Everyone freezes. Grace sets down her tea.

GRACE
Daniel. The box.

MEI-LING
He's just stopping by.

DANIEL
I'm not.

He opens the box. A ring. Mei-Ling presses the seam flat with her thumb. Holds it. Holds it longer.

FOREMAN
(from the loft)
Do we light them or not?

MEI-LING
Not yet.

She sets the lantern on the table, turns, and takes the ring from the box. Then she walks to the window, holds it up to the orange light, and the room waits. Fen drops her paper. Jasper stares at his shoes. Grace smiles into her tea.

The Foreman strikes a match.
